    "result_text": "The full suite (AcceptanceTest, the only test class) is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the telephone-uniqueness rule for `POST /api/owners`, rejecting a create with **409** when the normalized telephone already belongs to another owner.\n\nRather than growing any existing function or class, I added the behaviour as small, cohesive new units and inserted one step into the pipeline:\n\n**New files (each a single small method, so no surrounding class complexity to hold):**\n- `RejectDuplicateOwnerTelephone.java` \u2014 the new pipeline step; reads the already-normalized `OwnerFieldsDto` and throws `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException` if any existing owner shares the telephone.\n- `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException.java` \u2014 checked exception (so it appears in the `throws` clause per the escalation convention).\n- `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT`.\n- `...escalation/...DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException.yml` \u2014 wires the handler.\n\n**One edit to existing config (no code function touched):**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `rejectDuplicate` step between `normalize` and `build`.\n\nPlacement after `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` means the comparison uses the bare 10-digit value, and running before `build`/`save` (outside the write transaction's mutating steps) means a duplicate short-circuits to the escalation handler without touching the datastore. Both acceptance tests (`coreRejectsDuplicateTelephone`, `functionalityAllowsDistinctTelephone`) pass, along with the full suite.",
